    "turn_text": "country and decisions that impact the whole world, you really don't want those to be selfish decisions. And that's why I think faith is such an incredibly important part of someone's journey. I mean, outside of, you know, salvation and eternity, even just from a secular, a temporal perspective, while we're on this planet, I think you make a lot better decisions if you understand that it isn't just about you. And the the Hasidic Jews have a tradition whereby they have two slips of paper in their pockets, one in the right and one in the left. And one of the pockets says, for you, the whole universe is created. And in the other pocket, it says, you are but dust. And it's interesting, sort of this duality, and of course, those are the Hasidic Jews, but that kind of nomenclature, that kind of framework certainly exists in the Christian faith as well. Yeah, the whole universe was created for us. I mean, God gave His only begotten Son and had Him crucified to wash away our sins. I mean, that is sort of a breathtakingly large gift. On the other hand, we are but dust. Right? I mean, we are without, you know, without walking through that door, without accepting Jesus' grace, we're nothing. I mean, we're profoundly flawed and we're really not capable of anything meaningful. And I think that understanding that, I mean, having members of Congress understand the sort of massive scope of the impact that they can have on people's lives, while also understanding the kind of humility they need to have, how they are in some ways nothing. They're nothing special. That sort of servant leader approach I think is incredibly powerful.",
