    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "Well, this is probably off the beaten path. And I don't know that he provides me insight into how I should vote. But I keep coming back to the story of Zacchaeus and Luke with more frequency than one might expect. It's not very long. Again, I don't think most people think that it holds some sort of great wisdom and insight for how to govern a nation. But I mean, you just think about Zacchaeus, who's all of us, right? I mean, he's He's not particularly impressive. He's short. He's a tax collector. He's filled with sin. And like a lot of people, as they view the public square as governmental stuff, he doesn't really want to wait in, right? He's sort of on the outskirts of this crowd. He climbs up into a tree. He's kind of distant from the throng that is around Jesus. But Jesus does what all people in government should do which is really reaches out, right? I mean he says get down there, right? I mean don't don't be apart from this discussion, you know join into it and let's go to your house Zacchaeus and And of course Zacchaeus ultimately ends up giving away half his things to the poor right once he's engaged I mean in for a penny in for a pound and And Americans who think government is, you know, icky or angry or rancorous or not for them, we need to invite them into this discussion. Because once we unlock that potential, they could have as big an impact in their community as the key has had on his.",
